T01: TIMESHARING CLOCK TEST (IOT_32)
   2:           /*
   3:            * T01.am1 -- Comprehensive test of the BBN timesharing clock IOT.
   4:            *
   5:            * No external harness required.  User interaction:
   6:            *   Results are printed on the typewriter as each test completes.
   7:            *   Press any key after the summary to re-run all tests.
   8:            *
   9:            * IOTs exercised:
  10:            *   rck  (720032 / IOT 32)   -- read 1ms counter
  11:            *   cls  (722032 / IOT 2032) -- set clock parameters via AC
  12:            *   cct  (722132 / IOT 2132) -- set countdown timer via AC
  13:            *   cks  (720033)            -- read CKS status bits (checks cctcks flag)
  14:            *   esm  (720055)            -- enable sequence break master
  15:            *   cbs  (720056)            -- clear all pending SBS breaks
  16:            *
  17:            * Tests:
  18:            *   T01-1  rck returns 0 when clock is disabled
  19:            *   T01-2  rck returns non-zero after enabling clock and waiting
  20:            *   T01-3  Counter advances monotonically between two consecutive reads
  21:            *   T01-4  Disabling clock causes rck to return 0 again
  22:            *   T01-5  Countdown timer fires and sets cctcks flag (poll via cks)
  23:            *   T01-6  Countdown timer completion pulse: cct i blocks until countdown fires
  24:            *   T01-7  Countdown reset (count=0) clears cctcks status flag
  25:            *   T01-8  32ms SBS interrupt fires within the expected time window
  26:            *
  27:            * SBS setup notes for T01-8 (SBS256, non-SBS16 mode):
  28:            *   The SBS break sequence saves three words and then begins executing
  29:            *   at the fourth consecutive location.  For SBS256, that fixed frame
  30:            *   always starts at address 0:
  31:            *     loc 0: SBS saves old AC here
  32:            *     loc 1: SBS saves packed(ov1, exd, PC) here
  33:            *     loc 2: SBS saves IO here
  34:            *     loc 3: execution begins here (interrupt handler entry point)
  35:            *   Return from interrupt: 'jmp i 1' reads the saved PC from loc 1 and
  36:            *   acts as a DEBREAK, clearing the held-break state.
  37:            *
  38:            *   T01-8 assumes sbs16 has NOT been enabled in a prior program.  If it
  39:            *   has (sbs16 is sticky once set), channel 0 of SBS16 still uses the
  40:            *   same locations 0-3, so the handler entry and return idiom are
  41:            *   identical; however SBS16 also requires b1 bit 0 to be armed via
  42:            *   'asc 0' before ESM for the break to be queued.
  43:            *
  44:            * Assembler: am1 v1.28+
  45:            * Assemble:  am1 T01.am1
  46:            */

File /home/wje/PDP-1/pidp1-mods/Am1Includes/CLOCK/clockdefs.ah
   4:               // Defines for the IOT_32 timesharing clock
   5:               // Use as iot rck, etc.
   6:               // rck - read 1ms clock
   7:               // cls - set clock parameters
   8:               // cct - set countdown timer
  14:               // Flags in AC for cls, not compatible with macro, only macro1
  15:               // clss16 - enable SBS16, can't disable
  16:               // clsena - enable clock, must be set to use, including clr
  17:               // clsimn - enable minute interrupt
  18:               // clsims - enable 32ms interrupt
  25:               // And the channel masks
  26:               // clfmmn - minute channel
  27:               // clmffs - 32 millisecond channel
  31:               // NOTE for cct, enable SBS16 via iot cls cls16.
  32:               // Flags in AC for cct, not compatible with macro, only macro1
  33:               // cctie - enable interrupt
  37:               // And the channel and timer masks
  38:               // cctich - interrupt channel
  39:               // cctcnt - countdown timer
  43:               // The cks flag for the countdown timer
